---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const idea =
  "A solar buoy that sits on the water, measures a few stats, uses cellular internet to send them to a website you host.";
---

<Base title="AquaSense — one buoy, one website">
  <section class="hero">
    <div>
      <p class="kicker">Open hardware · Florida water · you host the site</p>
      <h1>AquaSense</h1>
      <p class="lede">{idea}</p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn" href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}build/`}>Build the kit</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}build/bom/`}>Bill of materials</a>
      </div>
      <p class="muted" style="margin-top:1.25rem;max-width:36rem">
        We do not run a cloud. GitHub Pages is documentation and a <em>simulated</em> dashboard.
        Live numbers come from <code>docker compose up</code> on a machine you control.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <img src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}images/hero-buoy.png`} alt="Photoreal render of a 24-inch PVC-square AquaSense buoy: Pelican case, solar on the lid, sensor cage in Florida water at dusk" />
      <p class="caption">Generated photoreal of the BOB-style hull — not a field photo. Parts count: labeled isometric and exploded SVG.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What it measures</h2>
    <p class="muted">One HTTPS POST every 15 minutes. No turbidity — the cheap analog probe is not waterproof and is not NTU.</p>
    <div class="grid-3" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
      <div class="card"><h3>Temperature</h3><p>Waterproof DS18B20 (DFRobot KIT0021). Digital 1-Wire on GPIO 18.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h3>pH</h3><p>SEN0169-V2 industrial analog transmitter. 0–3.0 V into GPIO 32. Two-point buffers on the desk.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h3>Conductivity → salinity</h3><p>DFR0300-H K=10, 10–100 mS/cm. PSS-78 at atmospheric pressure (UNESCO 44 / Fofonoff &amp; Millard 1983).</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h3>Dissolved oxygen</h3><p>SEN0237-A galvanic. mg/L from the transmitter; % saturation from Benson &amp; Krause 1984.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h3>Depth</h3><p>SparkFun MS5803-14BA. Gauge pressure × 0.01 ≈ metres of water.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h3>Where / power</h3><p>L76K GPS, LTE RSSI, battery millivolts. The board sleeps between wakes so a 10 W panel and a cell last overnight.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Your buoy, your website</h2>
    <p class="muted">Wake every 15 minutes. CSV on the SD card if LTE fails. The ingest URL is yours.</p>
    <img src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}images/architecture.svg`} alt="Diagram: buoy HTTPS POST to your ingest, Postgres, dashboard" style="margin-top:1rem;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--line)" />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The hull</h2>
    <p class="muted">24″ PVC pipe square (Levin BOB float), Pelican 1120, 10 W panel on the lid, five sensors in a cage. Gravity cables — no custom PCB.</p>
    <img src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}images/buoy-isometric.png`} alt="Labeled isometric of the PVC square buoy with Pelican, solar on lid, and five sensors" style="margin-top:1rem;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--line)" />
    <p class="caption">Exactly five sensors: pH, EC, DO, DS18B20, MS5803. Solar sits on the case lid, not under a second box.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Cost, honestly</h2>
    <p>
      A YSI EXO2 sonde lists around <strong>$7,300–$8,450</strong> before sensors
      (<a href="https://www.ysi.com/exo2">YSI EXO2</a>,
      <a href="https://www.fondriest.com/ysi-exo2-multiparameter-sonde.htm">Fondriest</a>).
      A NexSens-class data buoy is another few thousand.
      This kit’s CSV sums to about <strong>$866</strong> of street prices — check the linked pages; they move.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">AquaSense has not been in a lake. Treat every number as a kit you calibrate, not a certified station.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Nine steps</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}build/bom/`}>Buy the list</a> — every row has a Buy button.</li>
      <li><a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}build/host/`}>Stand up your website</a> — Docker, then the simulator.</li>
      <li><a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}build/flash/`}>Flash the board</a> — USB-A to USB-C, PlatformIO.</li>
      <li><a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}build/config/`}>Config the SD card</a> — your ingest URL and token.</li>
      <li><a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}build/calibrate/`}>Calibrate on the desk</a> — buffers, NaOH safety first.</li>
      <li><a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}build/wire/`}>Wire the box</a> — colors match the drawing.</li>
      <li><a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}build/hull/`}>Build the hull</a> — PVC, glands, solar, cage.</li>
      <li><a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}build/bucket/`}>Bucket test</a> — one real POST to localhost.</li>
      <li><a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}build/water/`}>Put it in the water</a> — permits, mooring, last-seen.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</Base>
